Let $
u$ be a complex measure on $(X,\mathcal A)$. Then the set functions
$$\operatorname{Re}
u(E):=\operatorname{Re}(
u(E)),\qquad \operatorname{Im}
u(E):=\operatorname{Im}(
u(E))$$
are finite signed measures on $(X,\mathcal A)$, and
$$
u(E)=\operatorname{Re}
u(E)+i\,\operatorname{Im}
u(E)\qquad(E\in\mathcal A).$$

derivations.claim:
Suppose now that $
u$ is complex. If $
u\ll\mu$ and $\mu(E)=0$, then $
u(E)=0$, so both $\operatorname{Re}
u(E)$ and $\operatorname{Im}
u(E)$ vanish; hence $\operatorname{Re}
u\ll\mu$ and $\operatorname{Im}
u\ll\mu$. Conversely, if both real and imaginary parts are absolutely continuous, then [L3] gives $
u(E)=0$ on every $\mu$-null set, so $
u\ll\mu$.

