derivations.claim:
By [L1] and [L2], the signed measures $\operatorname{Re}
u$ and $\operatorname{Im}
u$ are absolutely continuous with respect to $\mu$. By [L5], partition sums for their total variations are bounded by those of $|
u|$, so both have finite total variation. Choose an increasing exhaustion $(X_n)$ with $\mu(X_n)<\infty$; it is then common to $\mu$ and both signed measures. Applying [L3], choose real-valued $u,v\in L^1(\mu)$ such that $$\operatorname{Re}
u(E)=\int_E u\,d\mu,\qquad \operatorname{Im}
u(E)=\int_E v\,d\mu\qquad(E\in\mathcal A).$$

Put $h:=u+iv$. Then [L4] gives $h\in L^1(\mu)$, and for every measurable $E$ one has $$
u(E)=\operatorname{Re}
u(E)+i\,\operatorname{Im}
u(E)=\int_E u\,d\mu+i\int_E v\,d\mu=\int_E h\,d\mu.$$ If $h'$ is another such density, then its real and imaginary parts give alternative signed densities for $\operatorname{Re}
u$ and $\operatorname{Im}
u$, so the uniqueness part of [L3] makes $h'=h$ $\mu$-almost everywhere.

